#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:85
msgid ""
"From this result, we can determine that the axes explain approximately 58%, "
"29%, and 13% of the total variance in the points, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:88
msgid ""
"Let's compare this to the proportions of the known radii of the ellipsoid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:93
msgid ""
"Note how the two ratios are similar, but do not match exactly. This is "
"because the points of the ellipsoid are prolate and are denser near the "
"poles. If the points were normally distributed, however, the proportions "
"would match exactly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:98
msgid ""
"Create an array of normally distributed points scaled along the x-y-z axes. "
"Use the same scaling as the radii of the ellipsoid from the previous "
"example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:109
msgid ""
"Once again, the axes have ones along the diagonal as expected since the "
"points are already axis-aligned. Now let's examine the standard deviation "
"and compare the relative proportions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/_autosummary/pyvista.principal_axes.rst:122
