#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:4
msgid "Geometry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:5
msgid ""
"PyVista includes several functions to generate simple geometric objects. The"
" API reference for these functions is on this page. For additional details "
"see :ref:`create_geometric_objects_example` example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:9
msgid ""
"PyVista provides both geometric objects and geometric sources. These two "
"types serve similar but distinct purposes. Both allow you to create various "
"types of geometry, but they differ mainly in their level of abstraction and "
"usage scenarios."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:14
msgid ""
"If you are looking for an easy and Pythonic way to generate geometric "
"shapes, PyVista's geometric objects may be more appropriate. If you need "
"more control over the geometry or are building a complex VTK pipeline, then "
"using geometric sources would be more suitable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:21
msgid "Geometric Objects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:22
msgid ""
"High-Level Abstraction: Geometric objects like Box, Circle, Arrow, etc., in "
"PyVista are high-level Pythonic wrappers around VTK's underlying geometric "
"constructs. They provide a more user-friendly way to create geometries and "
"might include additional utility functions or attributes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:26
msgid ""
"Self-contained: Geometric objects are often intended to be stand-alone "
"entities, fully encapsulating the properties of the shape they represent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:28
msgid ""
"Quick Prototyping: These are often used for quick and simple tasks where "
"complex control over the geometry is not required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/geometric.rst:30
msgid ""
"Specific to PyVista: While they may be built on top of VTK, these high-level"
" abstractions might be unique to PyVista and not directly translatable to "
"raw VTK code."
msgstr ""

