#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:30
msgid ""
"A colormap is considered *perceptually uniform* if its color gradients are "
"evenly spaced, meaning equal steps in data produce equal perceptual changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:33
msgid ""
"Some colormap libraries assess perceptual uniformity using only the "
"lightness component (:math:`L^*` in the Lab color space), while others also "
"consider chromatic components (:math:`a^*` and :math:`b^*`). For the "
"colormaps on this reference page, both lightness *and* color differences "
"must meet the uniformity criteria to earn the :material-"
"regular:`visibility;2em;sd-text-info` label."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:39
msgid "Specifically, a colormap is labeled as perceptually uniform if:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:41
msgid ""
"The cumulative change in lightness (:math:`L^*` from CAM02-UCS) progresses "
"linearly. See `lightness of matplotlib colormaps "
"<https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/colors/colormaps.html#lightness-"
"of-matplotlib-colormaps>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:43
msgid ""
"The cumulative color difference (:math:`\\Delta E`, using the CIEDE2000 "
"metric) progresses linearly. See `Fig. 3c: cumulative color lightness "
"difference <https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-020-19160-7/figures/3>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:46
msgid ""
"Linearity is defined as having a coefficient of determination (:math:`R^2`) "
"greater than 0.99 when fitted with linear regression. When choosing a "
"colormap, those with the :material-regular:`visibility;2em;sd-text-info` tag"
" should be preferred over those with the :material-"
"regular:`visibility_off;2em;sd-text-warning` tag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:54
msgid ""
"Many of the ``matplotlib`` colormaps such as ``jet`` are not perceptually "
"uniform and should be avoided where possible, since these colormaps can "
"generate misleading visualizations. Colormaps from ``colorcet`` and "
"``cmocean``, and ``cmcrameri`` are therefore generally recommended over "
"those from ``matplotlib``. See `the misuse of colour in science "
"communication <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-19160-7>`_ and `testing "
"perceptual uniformity "
"<https://colorcet.holoviz.org/user_guide/Continuous.html#testing-perceptual-"
"uniformity>`_ for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:63
msgid ""
"Refer to the flowchart in the following dropdown for guidance on how to "
"choose a colormap."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:0
msgid "Guideline for choosing the right scientific colormap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:70
msgid ""
"Figure from Crameri, F., Shephard, G.E., & Heron, P.J. (2020). The misuse of"
" colour in science communication. Nature Communications, 11, 5444. "
"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-020-19160-7. Licensed under CC BY 4.0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../pyvista/doc/source/api/utilities/named_colormaps.rst:76
msgid ""
"Some colormap names such as ``gray`` and ``rainbow`` are duplicated across "
"packages, but have slight variations in the mapped colors. When a name "
"exists in ``matplotlib``, ``matplotlib``'s colormap is used so that plots "
"remain reproducible regardless of which optional colormap packages are "
"installed. Use a package-specific alias (for example an ``_r`` variant or a "
"distinct name from ``colorcet``, ``cmocean``, or ``cmcrameri``) to "
"explicitly request a third-party colormap."
msgstr ""

